   "text": "The pattern is best explained by two phases: BaCa2Mg(SiO4)2 (mp-1103554) and BaLaEuSbO6 (mp-1520390). Peak positions were read by counting along the 0.05\u00b0 grid, so they are good to about \u00b10.05\u00b0.\n\n**BaCa2Mg(SiO4)2 (trigonal, a = 5.485 \u00c5, c = 6.897 \u00c5).** Its calculated reflections match most of the sharp peaks. They all sit about 0.15\u20130.35\u00b0 higher than calculated, consistent with a lattice about 0.7% smaller.\n\n| Reflection | Calculated (\u00b0) | Observed (\u00b0) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 100 | 18.7 | 18.8 (weak) |\n| 101 | 22.7 | 22.85 |\n| 102 | 32.05 | 32.3 |\n| 110 | 32.6 | 32.85 (strongest) |\n| 111 | 35.2 | 35.45 |\n| 201 | 40.1 | 40.4 |\n| 112 | 42.06 | ~42.35 (weak) |\n| 202 | 46.4 | 46.75 |\n| 300 | 58.2 | ~58.6 |\n\n**BaLaEuSbO6 (distorted double perovskite, pseudo-cubic cell edge about 4.3 \u00c5).** The silicate does not explain the strong broad band at 29.0\u201330.3\u00b0 or the broad peaks near 41.65\u00b0 and 51.2\u201351.8\u00b0. The perovskite main reflections fit these, with the observed positions slightly lower than calculated. The breadth fits the reflection splitting expected from its slightly unequal cell axes.\n\n| Reflection | Calculated (\u00b0) | Observed (\u00b0) |\n|---|---|---|\n| 110-type | ~29.3 | 29.0\u201330.3 (broad) |\n| 200-type | ~42.0 | ~41.65 (broad) |\n| 211-type | ~52.0 | 51.2\u201351.8, overlapping silicate 210 |\n| 310-type | ~68.6 | weaker features near 68\u00b0 (less certain) |\n\n**Rejected candidates.**\n- **ErFeO3:** its 200 reflection near 33.8\u00b0 is absent.\n- **Sr2MnMoO6:** it would put its main peaks near 22.0\u00b0 and 31.3\u00b0.\n- **Dy2CuO4:** it would give a peak near 24.2\u00b0 that is not seen.\n- **NaCu3O4, CuAgO2, NaLuO2:** their calculated peak positions do not match the 29\u201330\u00b0 band.\n- **Cs2CeO3:** not checked in detail.\n\n{\"present\": [\"mp-1103554\", \"mp-1520390\"], \"confidence\": 0.7, \"note\": \"Sharp peaks index to trigonal BaCa2Mg(SiO4)2 with a small lattice shift, while the broad 29-30, 41.7 and 51.5 degree features fit the main reflections of the distorted double perovskite BaLaEuSbO6.\"}",
